<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Adolescent Sexual Behavior and Health in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00052</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>280988a1-6ff3-436d-9888-b6f0d9ecfa87</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Describe current statistics on adolescent sexual behavior</item>
    <item>Explain factors related to early first sexual intercourse</item>
    <item>Describe numerous aspects of adolescent sexuality</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Billy and Caroline were both 16 when they met and have been in a relationship for two years. They are each other’s first sexual partners and have been enjoying weekly sex for the past year. Caroline began taking the birth control pill a few months after they started having sex, and they agreed to use external condoms as well as a backup method of contraception and to prevent contracting any sexual transmitted infections. Recently, however, Billy calls Caroline less often and the frequency of sexual activity has also declined.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">Billy and Caroline’s experience is not uncommon in Western countries. During puberty and the adolescent period, many teens become curious about their bodies and begin experimenting with sexual behaviors. This scenario highlights certain topics that the chapter will cover, such as early sexual experience, birth control, sex education, and STIs. Also, social and romantic facets are also a part of adolescent sexual health for most teenagers.</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Adolescent Sexual Behaviors</title>
    <para id="para-00004">Adolescent sexuality involves the exploration of the motivational and functional components of sexuality such as sexual desire, sexual arousal, and sexual function, that are critical to understanding adult sexual life. Adolescent sexuality can be better understood with the biopsychosocial model, wherein biological factors include genetics and neuro-endocrinal factors; social/ environmental factors include the attitude of the parents toward sexuality, parenting style, peer relationship, media, and cultural influences; lastly, psychological factors consider one’s temperament, personality, gender identity, and attitudes towards sex (Kar et al., 2015).</para>
    <para id="para-00005">Adolescence is also an important time of self-exploration, including different types of pleasurable sexual practices (such as masturbation). During this phase, teens are likely to engage in several risky sexual behaviors such as unprotected penile-vaginal sex (Thoma et al., 2013), multiple sexual partners (Kann et al., 2011), and intercourse under the influence of alcohol or drugs (Rosario et al., 2014). Hence, adolescent sexuality can lead to several undesirable consequences as well, including emotional distress, the risk of contracting sexually transmitted diseases (STDs), an unplanned pregnancy, and sexual assault.</para>
    <para id="para-00006">The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) has researched adolescent behaviors for several decades using a survey called the Youth Risk Behavior Surveillance System (YRBSS). Data are collected every two years on a nationally representative sample of more than 10,000 U.S. high school students in grades 9 through 12. The questionnaires are answered anonymously, and results for individual years are reported as well as trends that emerge across multiple years. This data provides a comprehensive picture of mental and physical health-related behaviors in teens.</para>
    <para id="para-00007">In 2021, the most recent administration of the YRBSS, 30% of all high school students reported having ever had sexual intercourse (CDC, 2021). Females and males reported very similar rates (31% and 30%, respectively), and this pattern held true across ethnoracial groupings, except for Asian-American teens, with only 11% responding they had ever had sex. Further, LGBQ+ teens reported having had sex at a slightly higher but not significantly different rate than their heterosexual peers (33% vs. 29%, respectively).</para>
    <para id="para-00008">When we look at the 10-year trend, we see a marked and steady decline from 2011, when the proportion of high school students who had ever had sex was 49%, to 30% in 2021. This means more than two-thirds of high school students have not had sexual intercourse yet. When asked to report whether they are currently sexually active, 21% of teens reported in the affirmative. Note that the word “intercourse” was not defined to the high school students who completed the survey, so they may have varied understandings of what counts as sexual activity or intercourse. For our purposes, intercourse is defined as an act of sexual union, usually the insertion of the penis into the vagina followed by ejaculation. Variations include, among others, <emphasis effect="italics">coitus a tergo</emphasis>, in which the penis is inserted from the rear, and <emphasis effect="italics">coitus inter femora</emphasis> (interfemoral sex), in which the penis is inserted between the pressed thighs of the partner (APA, 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Contrary to YRBSS results, about 55% of boys and girls have had sex by the age of 18, and around 80% of them used contraception the first time. Among girls aged 15-19, 42% have had sex, and for boys in the same age group, the number is 44%. Moreover, researchers have found that teenagers who engage in frequent discussions with their peers about sex tend to have more favorable perceptions regarding the social advantages (such as increased popularity through sexual activity) and enjoyment linked to sex, while being less inclined to anticipate social consequences (like stigma or loss of self-esteem) associated with it (Ragsdale et al., 2014; Bleakley et al., 2018). However, there is likely underreporting on surveys as many adolescents may underestimate the behaviors that count as sex or may have never had intercourse defined to them (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_02_SexActive.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00010">Six percent of respondents to the 2021 YRBSS indicated they had had four or more sexual partners so far, also down sharply from the 2011 figures (18% for males, 13% for females). Of those who reported being sexually active, only 52% reported using a condom during their last intercourse, 33% reported using hormonal birth control (the pill), and only 10% reported effectively using both condoms and hormonal birth control together. The most used contraceptive method among teens between 2011-2015 remained the condom (reported by 97% of teen females), followed by withdrawal (60%) and the pill (56%) (Harper et al., 2018). These low rates increase the risks for unplanned teenage pregnancy and for spreading a sexually transmitted infection (STI). A <term id="term-00001">sexually transmitted infection (STI)</term> is caused by a virus, bacteria, fungus, or parasite that can be spread through sexual contact. Many STIs show no symptoms, so individuals might have an infection without realizing it. When an STI leads to noticeable symptoms, it is called a <term id="term-00002">sexually transmitted disease (STD)</term> (CDC, 2023).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00011">It’s not just about whether teens are having sex. Although STIs can be frightening and embarrassing, they are fairly common. The positive news is that most STIs can be cured or treated. If you’re sexually active, protect yourself by using condoms and getting tested for STIs. Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104STIs">video about sexually transmitted infections</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00012">The YRBSS also asked teens to report whether they had ever been forced to have sex or had ever experienced any kind of sexual violence (including unwanted touching and kissing). With an overall level of 11% reporting such an experience, fully 18% of female adolescents have experienced any form of sexual violence (and 5% of males), and a higher proportion of American Indian/Alaskan Native youth (16%), multiracial youth (15%), and LGBQ+ youth (22%) reported they had. Figures for these negative experiences have been steady for about 10 years, with a slight rise leading into the 2021 survey administration.</para>
    <note class="intersections-contexts" id="note-00002">
    <title>Affirmative Consent</title>
    <para id="para-00013">Consent culture refers to a societal framework that prioritizes and promotes mutual agreement, respect, and communication in all types of interpersonal interactions, particularly those of a sexual nature. In a consent culture, individuals are encouraged to seek and provide explicit and affirmative consent before engaging in any activity or behavior that may affect others. It emphasizes the understanding that consent must be freely given, reversible, informed, enthusiastic, and specific to each interaction. It aims to create an environment where all individuals feel empowered to assert their boundaries and where consent is valued as a fundamental aspect of healthy relationships and interactions. This shift towards advocating for affirmative consent, often known as "yes-means-yes" approaches, aims to foster open communication between partners and reduce instances of sexual assault (Jozkowski, 2015; Im et al., 2021). These approaches acknowledge affirmative consent as a clear and voluntary agreement, communicated either verbally or nonverbally, among all parties involved in sexual activity (Willis &amp; Jozkowski, 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00014">A recent study examined the adolescents’ views regarding affirmative consent within a sample of 226 high school students (comprising 58% female; 46% White, 24% Black, 25% Hispanic) from the southeastern United States. The study also explored the correlations between attitudes towards affirmative consent and factors such as gender, beliefs about gender roles, and sexual activity status. Furthermore, researchers investigated whether beliefs about gender roles acted as a mediator in the association between gender and attitudes towards affirmative consent. Overall, adolescents expressed favorable views towards affirmative consent. Females and teens with more egalitarian views on gender roles exhibited more positive attitudes towards affirmative consent compared to males and those with less egalitarian views on gender roles. Beliefs about gender roles were found to mediate the association between gender and attitudes towards affirmative consent (Javidi et al., 2021). These findings are consistent with previous research that noted college-aged women prefer a more explicit and affirmative consent process than college men (Muehlenhard et al., 2016).</para>
    <para id="para-00015">In summary, it is essential to explore adolescents' perspectives on affirmative consent, along with the underlying beliefs and encounters shaping these viewpoints, in order to comprehend the potential effectiveness of advocating for such practices within this demographic. Favorable perceptions of affirmative consent could potentially contribute to a reduction in sexual assault incidents among youth by minimizing misunderstandings between partners, providing clarity on legal definitions of sexual misconduct, and fostering sexual interactions characterized by enthusiasm and mutual agreement.</para>
    </note>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00016">In this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDHood">TED talk, Katie Hood discusses healthy relationships</link> and identifies five signs of an unhealthy relationship, whether it is with a romantic partner, a friend, or a family member. She emphasizes that, while love is an instinct and a natural emotion, we can all develop and enhance our ability to love better over time.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00017">Sexual behaviors other than intercourse have also been investigated among adolescents. According to CDC, 33% of adolescents (aged 15-17 years) have engaged in oral sex at least 1 time (CDC, 2020). Adolescents have a more favorable view of oral sex over vaginal intercourse, and many do not consider it to “count” as sex or see its potential for leading to STI transmission (Buhi &amp; Goodson, 2007; Strome et al., 2022). From these findings, oral sex is an important component of adolescent sexual experience and should receive attention from parents, educators, and healthcare professionals.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Predictors of Early First Sexual Intercourse</title>
    <para id="para-00018">Psychologists have focused on the individual-level and environmental factors that help predict and explain early first sexual intercourse (defined as occurring at age 14 or younger). The earlier that is, the more chances for unwanted negative consequences. For instance, self-reported data gathered from young adults found that adverse sexual events such as reproductive illness (i.e., any disease or disorder that affects the human reproductive system), infections, injuries, pregnancy termination or miscarriage, and future sexual challenges occur as some of the negative outcomes of an early first sexual intercourse. However, early sexual intercourse has also been associated with positive outcomes like healthier sexual function, including less pain during vaginal penetration, better orgasmic function, and reduced sexual inhibition (Peragine et al., 2022).</para>
    <para id="para-00019">Psychologists have identified many factors related to early first intercourse. Studies have consistently shown that it is linked with early pubertal timing (e.g., Mrug et al., 2014; Price &amp; Hyde, 2009), likely because early-maturing adolescents draw attention from older or similarly maturing peers. Data from U.S. 9<sup>th</sup>- and 11<sup>th</sup>-graders who completed the Minnesota Student Survey shows that the relationship between adverse childhood experiences and adolescent sexual risk behaviors and teen pregnancy is strong (Anda et al., 2006; Song &amp; Qian, 2020). A study of teenagers in Brazil found that both personal factors like living on the street (versus with families), repeating a school year, and using illegal drugs, as well as family factors like feeling less supported by parents and having more independence from the family, were linked to when teenagers started having sex (Zappe et al., 2018; Furlanetto et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00020">Parent–child closeness is another factor that fosters healthy adolescent development. When parents are distant, it creates an opportunity for adolescents to bond strongly with their peers, who may influence them negatively. A qualitative study done in South Africa found that lack of discipline and detached parenting had a negative influence on adolescent well-being and promoted early sexual behaviors (Anyanwu et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00021">Taking part in religious activities has been found to delay first sexual intercourse, especially for males. For instance, religiosity serves as a safeguard for abstaining from sexual activity among youth in Nigeria (Somefun, 2019). Individuals with high level of aggression and diagnoses of conduct disorders also tend to have sexual intercourse earlier (Fairchild et al., 2019). Finally, a number of family environment variables are also associated, including maternal education level, family composition, and family relationship dynamics. For example, in families with a high level of parental disapproval of sexual behavior, low parental monitoring of television viewing predicted earlier sexual activity (Reis, et al., 2023). It is also linked inversely to school achievement, which means struggling in school or not having strong academic background is a risk factor for early sexual intercourse (Lanari et al., 2020; Price &amp; Hyde, 2009; Wheeler, 2010).</para>
    <para id="para-00022">Thus, family and school environments and religious beliefs can modulate teen behaviors and sexual activities. Some protective factors against early sexual initiation include higher socioeconomic status, affiliation with a religious group, sex education, parental monitoring, and knowledge of reproductive health (Stephenson et al., 2014; Lara &amp; Abdo, 2016).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Adolescent Sexual Identity and Orientation</title>
    <para id="para-00023"><emphasis effect="bold">Sexual orientation</emphasis> is … <emphasis effect="bold">Sexual identity</emphasis> is a larger concept and consists of a person’s biological sex, gender identity, social sex-role, and sexual orientation (APA, 2023).</para>
    <para id="para-00024">The development of sexual orientation identity encompasses a series of changes and experiences over time, such as becoming aware, exploring, evaluating, committing, integrating, and communicating about one’s identity as a sexual being. This identity is influenced by their patterns of sexual attractions and actions. Sexual orientation is a multifaceted concept, covering an individual's comfort with various aspects of sexuality: sexual attraction, sexual behavior, romantic orientation, and sexual orientation identity (Mustanski et al., 2014; Hall, 2019).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00004">
    <para id="para-00025">Establishing your personal identity, and all of the beautiful components that make you unique, is a part of growth as a teenager and young adult. Self-discovery might include <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104PersonIdent">exploring gender identity and sexual orientation</link> as part of the process. Finding a supportive community is an important part of caring for your mental health along the way.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00026">The process of sexual orientation development for a LGBTQ youth commonly begins with ‘sensitization’ or the realization of same-sex attractions diverging from the conventional path of heterosexuality. This may lead to “identity confusion” due to societal stigma surrounding homosexuality. Subsequently, individuals may employ behavioral strategies to navigate the challenges of an identity crisis and social stigma, potentially adopting a “transitional” bisexual identity. Ultimately, this journey culminates in the commitment to an identity as gay, lesbian, or bisexual (Saewyc, 2011).</para>
    <para id="para-00027">Coming out” to family and peers can be challenging on many levels. Decades of research in the United States and abroad consistently show that sexual minority youth are at high risk for negative outcomes such as academic failure, emotional distress, compromised relationships, risky behavior, and suicidality (Russell &amp; Fish, 2016; Marshal et al., 2011). Young people with intersectional identities, such as Black, Indigenous, and people of color (BIPOC), individuals from low socioeconomic background, and displaced LGBTQ youth, are particularly vulnerable. These groups may have limited access to necessary services, due to barriers arising from a combination of their sexual and gender identity, ethnicity, and socioeconomic status (Salerno et al., 2020).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00005">
    <para id="para-00028">The Gender Unicorn: For decades, the queer and trans organizers have distinguished between gender identity, gender presentation/expression, sex, and attraction. Check out this site for more information https://transstudent.org/gender/</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Adolescent Childbearing</title>
    <para id="para-00029">The impact of adolescents bearing their own children is well documented and profound, and society’s concern about these pregnancies is justified. Adolescents themselves are not finished with development and are rarely prepared to begin raising another person. Indeed, adolescent parenthood hinders normative psychological development and can result in poorer psychological functioning.</para>
    <para id="para-00030">Pregnancy and childbirth significantly affect girls’ high school dropout rates. Approximately 50% of teenage moms complete a high school diploma before the age of 22, while about 90% of females who do not give birth as teenagers graduate from high school (Perper et al., 2010). Thus, teenage mothers are less likely to finish school. They may therefore attain a lower level of education, have lower earnings, and become more likely to use social welfare services (Gorry, 2019). Teenage mothers are also less likely to attend college and less likely to complete college when they do attend (Diaz &amp; Fiel, 2016). And overall, being a teenager mother brings emotional, psychological, and social difficulties, although not all teenage mothers have the same experiences (H.H.S., 2023). Having children may motivate some teen parents to complete their education to “find a better life” (Harden et al. 2006) and strive for self-sufficiency (Harding et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00031">Teenage fathers often are overlooked for several reasons: teen mothers are less likely to put fathers’ information on the birth certificate (Landry &amp; Forrest, 1995), teen males are less likely to affirm paternity as older fathers would (Paschal, 2013), and cultural norms dictate that mothers are the primary caretakers, so fathers may be studied less. Nonetheless, paternal involvement matters, and in fact, traits linked with young fatherhood might lead to situations that increase the chances of children engaging in risky sexual behavior and becoming teenage parents themselves (Bamishigbin et al., 2019). For instance, sons of teen fathers were 1.8 times more likely to become teenage fathers compared to sons of older fathers, even when other risk factors were taken into consideration (Sipsma et al., 2010).</para>
    <para id="para-00032">The good news is that the U.S. teen birth rate (births per 1,000 females aged 15 to 19 years) has been declining since 1991. It dropped from 17.4 per 1,000 females in 2018 to 15.4 in 2020, a record low (Martin et al., 2021). Rates vary significantly among ethnoracial groups, but the downward trend is occurring in all groups. In 2020, American Indian/Alaskan Native adolescent females had the highest birthrate, followed by Hispanic and Black adolescents and then White adolescents (CDC, 2017, 2022) (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>). About 75% of teenage pregnancies are unplanned (Finer &amp; Zolna, 2016).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_02_BirthRate.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00033">What accounts for these dramatic declines in adolescent childbearing rates? It is likely a combination of factors: greater access to and use of hormonal birth control and condoms, increased access to comprehensive sex education, access to family planning clinics, and an overall decline in sexual activity among teens (e.g., Lindbert, Wellings, &amp; Scott, 2020). Evidence-based teen pregnancy prevention programs, youth-friendly reproductive health services, and parental and community support can continue to reduce teen pregnancy (Goodreau et al., 2020). <term id="term-00003">Sex positivity</term> refers to individuals and communities that focus on openness, nonjudgmental attitudes, independence, and emancipation regarding sexuality and sexual expression (Donaghue, 2015). With a sex-positive approach, open discussions with parents and healthcare providers can help educate teens about their sexual desires and behaviors (Kågesten et al., 2016).</para>
    <note class="life-hacks" id="note-00006">
    <title>Sex-Positive Parenting Tips</title>
    <para id="para-00034">Creating a sex-positive climate in the home is an important aspect of contemporary parenting (Saady, 2022). The statements, behaviors, and attitudes that make up this kind of parenting don’t mean parents are encouraging their teenager to have sex. Rather, they recognize that sex and sexuality are a natural, healthy, and pleasurable part of life, and they set the stage for safe exploration, boundary setting, and open communication. Teaching about consent is another important part of this approach to sexuality.</para>
    <para id="para-00035">Like those who adopt the authoritative parenting style, parents who approach their teen’s emerging sexuality in a sex-positive way clearly communicate expectations for their teen regarding sexual behavior. When these expectations are not met, sex-positive parents communicate their disappointment and reinforce the agreed-upon parameters for future behavior. Shame and guilt are actively avoided.</para>
    <para id="para-00036">Here are some other sex-positive parenting tips and practices:</para>
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Use medically correct terms for sexual body parts and acts from early childhood onward</item>
    <item>Discuss options for birth control and STI prevention</item>
    <item>Discuss masturbation in healthy terms, including appropriate places to do it</item>
    <item>Focus on the functionality of body parts rather than on their appearance</item>
    <item>Model healthy sexuality with parents’ own adult partners, including showing the loving aspects of sex like hand-holding, kissing, and cuddling</item>
    <item>Have periodic and ongoing discussions about sex, tailored to the teen’s current knowledge and experience level</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00037">In these ways, parents acknowledge adolescents’ emerging sexuality and take an active part in setting their child up for positive decisions and experiences around sexuality.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Sex Education</title>
    <para id="para-00038">Sex education is an important tool not only in encouraging safe and healthy sexual behavior and choices, but also in preventing STIs and unplanned pregnancies.</para>
    <para id="para-00039">In the United States, there are three main types of sex education programs:</para>
    <list id="list-00003">
    <item><term id="term-00004">Abstinence-only education</term> teaches that you should wait until marriage to engage in sexual intercourse and usually doesn’t include information about using contraception or condoms to prevent pregnancy and STIs.</item>
    <item><term id="term-00005">Abstinence-plus education</term> also promotes waiting until marriage to have sex but provides information about contraception and condoms.</item>
    <item><term id="term-00006">Comprehensive sex education</term> gives accurate, age-appropriate information on topics like sexual development, behaviors (including abstinence), healthy relationships, life skills, communication, sexual orientation, and gender identity.</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00040">Current recommendations are to offer comprehensive sex education to adolescents in schools (Goldfarb &amp; Lieberman, 2021). Several scientific, governmental, and non-profit organizations in the United States have been at the forefront of establishing the core features of a comprehensive sex education curriculum. The National Sexuality Education Standards (NSES), released in 2012, include the following broad topics: healthy relationships (including consent), anatomy and physiology, puberty and sexual development, gender identity, sexual orientation, sexual health, and interpersonal violence (Future of Sex Education Initiative, 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00041">How effective is such educational programming? A review of more than 1,500 published studies on all aspects of comprehensive sex education concludes this approach is a resounding success across multiple outcome measures (Goldfarb &amp; Lieberman, 2021). The review highlights the importance of introducing such education as early in adolescence as possible (some say even as early as second or third grade (Lashof-Sullivan, 2015), carefully tailoring information to local needs and developmental appropriateness. In addition to helping to reduce adolescent childbearing and STI transmission, comprehensive sex education can increase empathy and respect for others, foster a positive self-image, instill a better sense of self-control and safety, lower levels of homophobia in a school, increase gender knowledge (i.e., knowledge and understanding of the concept of gender, the division of roles, and gender equality), and improve learning skills that lead to healthier relationships among teens. In short, it is an effective part of the conversation on adolescent sexuality. Combined with authoritative parenting techniques, it provides a firm foundation for healthy sexuality in adolescence and beyond.</para>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00007">
    <list id="list-00004">
    <item>Adolescent sexual behaviors encompass a range of experiences and expressions as individuals explore their sexuality. This period is marked by questioning and discovery, including feelings of attraction, orientation, and gender identity. Adolescents may engage in various behaviors, such as forming romantic relationships, exploring sexual activity, and seeking information about sexual health.</item>
    <item>Understanding and supporting adolescents in navigating their sexual identity and behaviors with access to comprehensive sex education and non-judgmental healthcare services are crucial for promoting healthy development and informed decision-making.</item>
    <item>Teen pregnancy factors are influenced by socio-economic status, early onset of puberty, access to comprehensive sex education, and cultural attitudes toward sexuality. Prevention strategies include promoting contraceptive use, providing access to reproductive healthcare services, and implementing educational programs addressing healthy relationships, consent culture, and responsible sexual behavior.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
